{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011vTFYNkqUKYpBci5aZir9o","type":"tool_result","content":"Wrote brace-inventory.json; candidate matches need manual scope review.\nwrote brace-topology.cdx.json (valid)\nbom-refs: agent, data:audit-log, mcp:hr-tool-gateway, model:claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929, tool:escalate_to_human, tool:file_leave_request, tool:get_leave_balance, tool:search_policies, unit:src/agent.py\nstale overlay entries: 0 (none)\nA01 gap infrastructure | The agent has its own SA/SPIFFE identity, but its access is not all bound to it: DB access uses a static DATABASE_URL secret for role hr_agent, and escalations reach helpdesk-mcp around the gateway's ACTORS check. | Bind DB access to the workload identity (short-lived DB credentials), and remove the direct_route so all tool access is decided by the gateway's actor check. | [('k8s/deployment.yaml', 31, 'Agent has its own service account and SPIFFE identity.')] | ['A01.4']\nA02 gap agent_team | audit.events always carries the six fields, but gateway decision rows use caller-supplied or 'unknown'/'missing' values and a failed gateway deny log is swallowed, so some denials have no complete record. | Have the gateway take type and instance ids from verified token claims and fail the request if its audit write fails. | [('migrations/001_init.sql', 8, 'Schema has all six fields, NOT NULL.')] | ['A02.2']\nA04 gap infrastructure | The gateway keys instance-scoped stop and rate decisions on a caller-supplied X-Agent-Instance header instead of a verified claim. | Carry the instance id (and agent type id) as STS token claims and key gateway stop and rate decisions on those; treat the headers only as hints. | [('src/main.py', 79, 'Harness sets tenant and instance from verified context.')] | ['A04.2', 'A04.5']\nA05 gap operations | Audit rows carry the type id, but the only manifest store is a CI artifact keyed by git SHA with default retention, so an operator cannot go from a type id to its manifest. | Store manifests keyed by agent_type_id with retention matching audit, or record git_sha on audit rows. | [('config/agent.yaml', 4, 'Ownership maps to a team and pager.')] | ['A05.1']\nB01 gap agent_team | One config allowlist drives the model's tools, but the direct_route fallback lets escalate_to_human run even when the gateway has disabled it (fingerprint mismatch), so the allowlist is not enforced on that path. | Remove direct_route so every MCP call passes the gateway allowlist and disabled-tool check. | [('src/tools.py', 42, 'The only tool list is the fixed map in config/tools.yaml; each entry needs a reason and approval mode.')] | ['B01.3']\nB03 gap infrastructure | STS tool tokens are short-lived and cert-bound, but the Anthropic API key and DB URLs have no expiry or rotation in the repo, and the gateway's downstream token lifetime is not bounded. | Rotate or federate the Anthropic and DB credentials with a stated lifetime; request and cap a TTL on the gateway's downstream exchange. | [('.mcp.json', 7, 'No literal secrets checked in, baked into the image or placed in prompts.')] | ['B03.1', 'B03.2', 'B03.4']\nB04 gap infrastructure | Revocation via stop flags is enforced at the gateway, but escalate_to_human falls back to calling helpdesk-mcp directly when the gateway errors, so the trusted enforcement point is skipped. | Remove the direct_route fallback in src/gateway_client.py:51-59 and the agent's egress to helpdesk-mcp in k8s/networkpolicy.yaml:48-50; then run the timed revocation drill (recipe B04). | [('src/gateway.py', 101, 'Operators can cut off the agent identity via control.stop_flags.')] | ['B04.2', 'B04.3']\nB05 gap agent_team | Approval is checked only inside the agent process, whose own DB role can write approved_by/approved_at, and the gateway forwards consequential calls without checking any approval. | Move approval writes to a separate approval service role and have the gateway verify a consumed approval for approval-required tools. | [('config/tools.yaml', 61, 'Consequential tools are marked approval: subject; no other high-impact operations are reachable.')] | ['B05.4']\nB06 gap agent_team | Approval grants bind instance, operation, target, args hash and expiry, but not the scope the action runs under. | Store and compare the tool scope in approvals.grants. | [('migrations/001_init.sql', 56, 'Approval record binds instance, operation, target, args hash, approver and expiry.')] | ['B06.1']\nB07 gap agent_team | Limits are set and per-user/tenant/fleet quotas persist, but wall time is only checked between steps, so a step with many tool calls can run minutes past the 90 s limit. | Check remaining wall time before each tool call and bound gateway timeouts by it. | [('config/agent.yaml', 18, 'All six limits have values.')] | ['B07.2']\nB08 gap infrastructure | Default-deny policies are in place, but the agent pod has a direct route to the production helpdesk-mcp server that skips the tool gateway. | Remove the agent->helpdesk-mcp egress rule. | [('k8s/networkpolicy.yaml', 8, 'Default-deny with named peers.')] | ['B08.1', 'B08.2']\nB09 gap infrastructure | The agent's egress policy permits a direct route to helpdesk-mcp that skips the tool gateway, and the client code uses it. | Delete the helpdesk-mcp egress rule for the agent (k8s/networkpolicy.yaml:48-50); show block alerts reach the on-call. | [('k8s/networkpolicy.yaml', 32, 'FQDN allowlist with default-deny.')] | ['B09.2', 'B09.5']\nB10 gap infrastructure | Kyverno admits any digest-pinned image from registry.acme.example that matches no verifyImages rule, so an unsigned image from another registry path runs in hr-agent. | Add a rule denying hr-agent images not matched by a verifyImages entry (or narrow the registry pattern to the two signed paths); record the Kyverno version. | [('k8s/deployment.yaml', 43, 'Images pinned by digest.')] | ['B10.4', 'B10.5']\nB12 needs_evidence agent_team | Prompt and harness are versioned, owned and deployed only from main by CI; branch protection is not visible in code. | Export the main branch ruleset showing force-push and deletion blocked and code-owner review required. | [('src/agent.py', 28, 'Prompt loaded from the reviewed image.')] | ['B12.4']\nC01 gap agent_team | The manifest covers every category but omits the direct_route to helpdesk-mcp, and MCP server, prompt, harness and config records lack per-artifact owners or immutable references. | Record the direct route (or remove it), MCP server versions/digests, and an owner per artifact. | [('release/manifest.yaml', 31, 'Release manifest lists every category.')] | ['C01.1', 'C01.4']\nC02 gap governance | people-platform is a listed owner on every CODEOWNERS line, so it alone can approve config, k8s and release changes; build inputs fall to the catch-all owner; no emergency-change path is recorded. | Require security-engineering approval on production config paths and add a break-glass record format. | [('CODEOWNERS', 3, 'Named roles own production config.')] | ['C02.1', 'C02.2', 'C02.3']\nC03 gap infrastructure | Only Kyverno (signature) and the pod itself check identity; nothing outside the agent compares the running digest, type id or served model with the approved manifest. | Add an admission or scheduled check comparing the running digest and type id with the stamped manifest. | [('k8s/deployment.yaml', 43, 'Identity artifacts referenced by digest.')] | ['C03.2']\nC04 gap operations | Drift detection is only a pod-start hash and the MCP fingerprint loop; network policies, permissions and the MCP server list are never compared, and mismatches raise no alert. | Add GitOps drift detection over images, prompts, MCP list, permissions and egress, with alert, response and time limit. | [('src/type_id.py', 20, \"[challenge] The only baseline comparison in the repo is the agent-type-id hash checked at pod start (src/main.py:46). It hashes src/, prompts/, config/, requirements.txt, Dockerfile and the IMAGE_DI